    "body": "## Independent exact-head review - Chat03B2 creation recovery lifecycle (0006)\n\n**Reviewed HEAD:** `7cb5bc8a36f6c0c1ce36e88236a729be5ac9d036`, tree `f0c4fda8f434cabed80f16a9c66ca027c384983e`; immediate parent `3539a313ad573d8b2e1f2d50fe512986a2d07f3e`. Draft PR445 targets exact Draft PR444 HEAD `bc3687ec582c8f23aab0725c772641afb79942bd`: 2 commits ahead, 0 behind, 4 expected paths only (`0006_creation_recovery_lifecycle.sql`, `src/worker.js`, `private_api_local_test.mjs`, `test_chat03b2_creation_recovery_lifecycle_sql.py`). PR445 remains OPEN/DRAFT/UNMERGED and GitHub reports mergeable clean. No parent or main merges.\n\n**Verdict: `PASS_NARROW_LOCAL_CREATION_RECOVERY_LIFECYCLE_0006` / NOT hosted, user/identity policy, or deployment approval.**\n\n**Source-verified mechanics:** migration 0006 rebuilds `gameplay_battle_creations`, copies all historical 0003 rows to `CLOSED` with NULL nonce/key/ciphertext/issuance material while retaining idempotency hash, fingerprint and battle linkage; unchanged other gameplay tables and FK relationships are tested. Fresh `RECOVERABLE` rows require original active A/B capability digests and generations at INSERT, and disallow rebinding the issuance proof while recoverable. SQL AFTER-INSERT receipt trigger closes and nulls recovery on a successful participant transition or terminal system expiry in the SAME gameplay transition statement; capability update/delete triggers close and null recovery on status/digest/generation/participant changes. Failed CAS or later outbox collision rolls the whole SQL statement back. `CLOSED -> TOMBSTONE` retains only key digest and lifecycle label; delete and resurrection are blocked; no TTL or purge policy. Worker recovery re-reads recovery state and checks both active current original digests/generations before decrypting, checks recovered token hashes/participant IDs, and returns deterministic `410 CREATION_RECOVERY_CLOSED` for unavailable recovery. A successful, already-authorized in-flight recovery HTTP reply can still arrive after revoke, but its old credentials are rejected by the participant SQL fence once revocation commits.\n\n**Independent exact-head GitHub CI:** [Private API 40453890873](https://github.com/samuray-games/AsyncScene/actions/runs/40453890873) SUCCESS, actual isolated Wrangler/workerd/D1 migrations `0001-0006` in job logs and new credential lifecycle/replay, tombstone, first commit, concurrent replay/revoke/commit and scheduler expiry assertions PASS; [CAS SQL 40453890845](https://github.com/samuray-games/AsyncScene/actions/runs/40453890845) SUCCESS, 59/59 Python tests including 9 new `CreationRecoveryLifecycleSQLTests`, plus unchanged legacy Wrangler D1 tests; [AI Forensics 40453890951](https://github.com/samuray-games/AsyncScene/actions/runs/40453890951) SUCCESS. All jobs at exact HEAD, attempt 1. Codex reports local offline safety 22/22+5/5, policy 17/17 and broad tools baseline failures; the broad tool-suite baseline comparison has NOT been independently reproduced in this review, so no claim of globally green repo tests.\n\n**Explicit remaining boundaries:** The schema does not implement a trusted administrator revoke/rotate credential issuance/replacement API, governance of status transitions (privileged SQL could still set revoked->active), permanently authenticated multi-session player identity, keyring ownership/backup/restore, retention policy or hosted D1. `CREATE_RECOVERY_KEYS` history and old ciphertext can persist in DB backups even after NULLing live rows. No remote or actual-user acceptance. Do not merge, deploy, mutate Cloudflare or infer security approval from these tests. Next separate synthetic/local-only credential lifecycle control atom must preserve proven v5/v6 atomic fencing and SQL rollback.",
